ters to model the world instead  of doing it ourselves so parsimonious or  sparse models are still important today  they have the property that by Road  ducing the amount of free variables or  parameters as much as possible to the  simplest possible explanation you tend  to prevent overfitting if I have a  million degrees of freedom that I get to  tweak a million parameters a very  complicated non parsimonious model if  I'm if I'm liberal with my parameters  then I might very well over fit and get  something that looks right on my data  but doesn't have the root cause kind of  encapsulated okay so parsimonious or  sparse models tend to over tend to  prevent overfitting I say sparse meaning  as few parameters or as few terms as  possible okay that's what we mean by  sparse these models generally are more  interpretable because again if I have  five parameters or five terms instead of  a million terms that's a lot less terms  to try to understand and to communicate  with other people to try to analyze okay  so sparse parsimonious models generally  are more interpretable they are the  models that require the fewest terms  needed to model the data that that's  what we mean by sparse or parsimonious  models and generally they're robust they  prevent overfitting they remain  interpretable this is what we want in  modeling okay so we're gonna talk about  sparsity and compression and the  mathematics of sparse vectors and sparse  arrays but I want you to remember why we  care so much about this aside from the  compression angle is that in machine  learning in the world of modeling with  data we again we still want parsimonious  models we still want to remember the  words of Einstein and Newton and Pareto  these are important we need to adhere to  Occam's razor when we model physical  systems okay so parsimonious or stingy  models are often the ones that I  personally personally want okay  thank you

